<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Barbara Sanseverino</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1550">1550</a>–<a href="/years/1612">1612</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Barbara Sanseverino, Countess of Sala</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Barbara Sanseverino, Countess of Sala, was an Italian noblewoman celebrated for her beauty and culture at the court of Parma, praised by poets including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torquato-tasso">Torquato Tasso</a>. Accused with members of the Sanvitale family and other nobles of conspiring against Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranuccio-i-farnese-duke-of-parma">Ranuccio I Farnese</a>, she was beheaded in Parma in May 1612, and the estates of the condemned were confiscated.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/barbara-sanseverino.jpg" alt="Barbara Sanseverino" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barbara_Sanseverino.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Barbara Sanseverino was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 1550, the daughter of Gianfrancesco and Lavinia Sanseverino. She moved to Parma on her marriage to Giberto IV Sanvitale, Lord of Sala Baganza. Their son Girolamo was invested on condition that he take the surname and coat of arms of the Sanseverino, a house of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. She was the confidante and mistress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vincenzo-i-gonzaga">Vincenzo I Gonzaga</a>, Duke of Mantua, who moved for a time from Mantua to Viadana for her in 1580. After her first husband's death she married Count Orazio Simonetta, Overlord of Torricella, and settled at Colorno.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Duke Ranuccio Farnese of Parma sought control of Colorno and began a lawsuit under feudal law, arguing that the Sanvitale investiture was void. Seeing the case go against them, the Sanvitale planned to remove him. Those drawn in, besides Barbara and Orazio Simonetta, included her son Girolamo and his son Gianfrancesco, Alfonso II Sanvitale, Count Girolamo da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-da-correggio">Correggio</a>, Count Alberto of Canossa and the Marquise of Grana Agnese del Carretto. The attack was to take place in the church of Fontevivo at the baptism of Ranuccio's nephew Alessandro, but a servant of Gianfrancesco, jailed and tortured on an unrelated matter, gave away names and the plot was uncovered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The inquisitorial trial under Judge Piossasco ended on 4 May 1612 with death sentences for all and the confiscation of their goods. On 19 May Barbara and nine others were beheaded on a scaffold near Piazza Garibaldi in Parma; the headsman failed to sever her neck with the axe and finished with a butcher's cleaver, then abused her body before the cheering crowd.</p><p>Ranuccio also accused Duke Ferdinando Gonzaga of Mantua and three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaspina">Malaspina</a> lords of paying for the plot, and a war was averted by the mediation of Pope Paul V, the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and Charles Emmanuel of Savoy. Torquato Tasso wrote a sonnet in praise of her hair, and in 1947 Alfredo Zerbini told her story in eighty sonnets in Parmigiano dialect, La congiura di Feudatäri.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Barbara Sanseverino” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ranuccio I (r. 1592–1622) was especially harsh: in 1612 he had a group of leading nobles, among them <b>Barbara Sanseverino, Countess of Sala</b>, executed for an alleged conspiracy against him, and their estates were confiscated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Parma, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.47,9.19&amp;year=1590" aria-label="Milan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4768 560 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Barbara Sanseverino"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5218 830h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5218 830h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.47,9.19&amp;year=1590">What was here in 1590? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
